---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/nav';
---

<Base title="Why — The Courtyard Block" current="why">
  <header class="mx-auto max-w-[920px] px-6 pt-20 sm:px-12">
    <p class="mb-4 font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-terracotta">
      The obstacle course
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-[clamp(40px,5.5vw,68px)] font-medium leading-[1.05] tracking-[-0.015em]">
      Why American cities don't look like this
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-[65ch] text-[22px] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80">
      Not culture, not taste, not weather. A stack of rules and habits, each reasonable-sounding
      alone, that together made the European default block impossible to build. Eight of them.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[920px] px-6 pt-14 sm:px-12">
    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">01</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">Euclidean zoning separated the uses</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">
        American zoning's founding move was to sort the city into single-use districts. Housing
        here, commerce there. On most of the American zoning map, an apartment above a shop — the
        single most ordinary condition in Berlin, Vienna, or Barcelona — is simply not a permitted
        use. The mixed block didn't lose a competition; it was defined out of the rulebook.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">02</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">The missing height band</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">
        US rules commonly allow two or three stories — or, in small downtown pockets, a tower. The
        band in between, five to seven stories at the sidewalk, is precisely where the European
        street wall lives: Berlin's Traufhöhe, Vienna's Gründerzeit cornice, the Paris boulevard.
        Call it the missing middle of <em>height</em>, not just of unit count.
      </p>
      <figure class="mt-8">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 900 300" class="block h-auto w-full" role="img" aria-label="Zoning cartoon: allowed at 2-3 stories, allowed as a tower, missing in the 5-7 story band">
          <line x1="40" y1="250" x2="860" y2="250" stroke="#1c1915" stroke-width="2.5"></line>
          <rect x="40" y="130" width="820" height="66" fill="rgba(180,74,47,0.1)"></rect>
          <g fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.1)" stroke="#1c1915" stroke-width="2">
            <rect x="80" y="212" width="70" height="38"></rect>
            <rect x="180" y="196" width="70" height="54"></rect>
          </g>
          <g fill="none" stroke="#b44a2f" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 5">
            <rect x="330" y="132" width="90" height="118"></rect>
            <rect x="450" y="132" width="90" height="118"></rect>
          </g>
          <rect x="640" y="40" width="64" height="210" fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.1)" stroke="#1c1915" stroke-width="2"></rect>
          <g font-family="'Source Sans 3',sans-serif" font-size="13" letter-spacing="2">
            <text x="80" y="278" fill="#1c1915">ALLOWED: 2–3 STORIES</text>
            <text x="330" y="278" fill="#b44a2f">MISSING: THE 5–7 STORY STREET WALL</text>
            <text x="620" y="278" fill="#1c1915">ALLOWED: THE TOWER</text>
            <text x="46" y="122" fill="#b44a2f">THE BAND EUROPE BUILT ITS CITIES IN</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <figcaption class="mt-3 font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-ink/55">
          Fig. 07 — The missing height band. Illustrative.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </article>

    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">03</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">Parking minimums ate the courtyard</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">
        Require one or two stalls per home and the middle of the block becomes a garage. The
        courtyard turns into a podium deck over parking; the ground floor becomes a ramp and a
        lobby; the sidewalk shrinks to feed curb cuts. Where minimums have been repealed — as
        California has done near transit — the courtyard becomes geometrically possible again.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">04</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">Setbacks and lot coverage dissolved the street wall</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">
        Front setbacks turn street walls into front yards; side setbacks slice the block into
        leftover strips no one uses. The European block inverts the diagram: build to the lot lines
        at the street, and pool the open space into one shared Hof in the middle, big enough to be
        worth something.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">05</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">The double-loaded corridor and the two-stair rule</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">
        American egress practice long required two enclosed stairs for buildings above a few
        stories, which pushes designs toward deep slabs with a sealed hallway down the middle. The
        single-stair, gallery-access mid-rise — the social heart of La Borda and R50 — was
        effectively illegal in most US cities. That is changing: several states have recently moved
        on single-stair reform, and the conversation is live in California. It is a code research
        item, not a fantasy — the current state-by-state picture, and what to do with it, is in
        <a href={withBase('/plan/')}>the plan</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">06</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">Sidewalks as leftover space</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">
        A Berlin or Vienna sidewalk is a designed room: wide enough for café tables, tree pits,
        bicycles, and two prams passing. Many American sidewalks are whatever remains after travel
        lanes and street parking are drawn. The type needs its front room back.
      </p>
      <figure class="mt-8">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 900 260" class="block h-auto w-full" role="img" aria-label="Street width comparison: US arterial mostly lanes, European street with wide sidewalks and tram">
          <g font-family="'Source Sans 3',sans-serif" font-size="13" letter-spacing="2" fill="#1c1915">
            <text x="40" y="30">A US ARTERIAL, ~80 FT</text>
            <text x="40" y="150">A EUROPEAN STREET, ~80 FT</text>
          </g>
          <g>
            <rect x="40" y="44" width="60" height="40" fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.12)"></rect>
            <rect x="100" y="44" width="90" height="40" fill="rgba(180,74,47,0.18)"></rect>
            <rect x="190" y="44" width="480" height="40" fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.28)"></rect>
            <rect x="670" y="44" width="90" height="40" fill="rgba(180,74,47,0.18)"></rect>
            <rect x="760" y="44" width="60" height="40" fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.12)"></rect>
          </g>
          <g font-family="'Source Sans 3',sans-serif" font-size="12" letter-spacing="1.5" fill="#1c1915">
            <text x="44" y="104">WALK 6'</text>
            <text x="104" y="104">PARK 9'</text>
            <text x="370" y="104" fill="#f6f1e8" transform="translate(0,-34)">FOUR TRAVEL LANES</text>
            <text x="674" y="104">PARK 9'</text>
            <text x="764" y="104">WALK 6'</text>
          </g>
          <g>
            <rect x="40" y="164" width="200" height="40" fill="rgba(63,92,69,0.3)"></rect>
            <rect x="240" y="164" width="170" height="40" fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.28)"></rect>
            <rect x="410" y="164" width="170" height="40" fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.16)"></rect>
            <rect x="580" y="164" width="240" height="40" fill="rgba(63,92,69,0.3)"></rect>
          </g>
          <g font-family="'Source Sans 3',sans-serif" font-size="12" letter-spacing="1.5" fill="#1c1915">
            <text x="44" y="224">SIDEWALK + TREES + CAFÉ, 20'</text>
            <text x="244" y="224">TRAM / BUS</text>
            <text x="414" y="224">ONE LANE + BIKES</text>
            <text x="584" y="224">SIDEWALK + TREES + MARKET, 24'</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <figcaption class="mt-3 font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-ink/55">
          Fig. 08 — Same right-of-way, different rooms. Widths illustrative.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </article>
    <article class="border-t border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">07</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">Finance has no box for it</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">
        Lenders and appraisers underwrite what they can compare: "garden apartment," "podium wrap,"
        "high-rise." A 40-unit limited-equity courtyard co-op with shops has no product box, so it
        reads as risk regardless of its actual economics. And a developer's profit wants sellable
        condos, not a Hof — which is why the European examples are almost all resident-led, with the
        developer's margin folded back into the building.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="border-t border-b border-ink/15 py-9">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">08</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-[30px] font-medium">What Berlin actually is</h2>
      <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-[65ch] text-[19px] leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">
        Not magic culture. A height cap that produces a continuous cornice line; perimeter blocks
        that enclose Hinterhöfe; trams in the street; ground-floor commercial as the unremarkable
        default; buildings that enclose space instead of sitting as objects in parking. Every one of
        those is a rule or a habit — and rules can be rewritten. Once the type is visible, the
        question stops being "what if we invented a new way to live?" and becomes "why not this
        lot?"
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto mt-24 max-w-[1240px] px-6 sm:px-12">
    <a href={withBase('/berkeley/')} class="block bg-ink px-12 py-14 text-paper no-underline hover:text-paper">
      <p class="font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[#e08b6d]">So: why not this lot?</p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[clamp(26px,3.4vw,42px)] font-medium leading-[1.2] text-paper">
        The Berkeley proposal →
      </p>
    </a>
  </section>
</Base>
